From: postmarketOS rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: rhodep: pin pm6125 L9 at 1.8 V

L9 was declared with the PMIC's whole range, 1504 mV to 2000 mV, and no
consumer asks for a particular voltage, so the framework settles on the
bottom of it. Measured on the device with Bluetooth on, which is what pulls
this rail up:

	l9    1 user   enabled   1504000 uV

Everything on L9 is a 1.8 V load. In this device tree it is the WCN3990's
vddio. In the vendor tree it is also the WCD937x's cdc-vdd-rxtx and
cdc-vddpx, the SAR sensor's cap_vdd, and on the boards that declare an NFC
supply at all, the NFC controller at 1800000 uV. The vendor sets it with
qcom,init-voltage = <1800000> in holi-regulators-pm6125.dtsi; mainline has
no such property, so the constraint itself has to say it.

Found while chasing the NFC controller, which does not ACK at 0x27. That
investigation is not concluded -- the chip still does not answer with the
rail up and VEN asserted -- but a 1.8 V part sitting on a 1.5 V rail is
worth correcting whether or not it turns out to be the reason.

Worth noting Bluetooth has been running on this rail at 1504 mV all along.
It works, so this is not the cause of any known fault, but it is out of
spec and the warm-reset trouble documented in
docs/bluetooth-warm-reset-wip/ lived on this same supply.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375-motorola-rhodep.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375-motorola-rhodep.dts
@@ -738,9 +738,22 @@
 			regulator-max-microvolt = <1304000>;
 		};
 
+		/*
+		 * 1.8 V, not the 1504 mV the bottom of the PMIC's range gives.
+		 *
+		 * Declaring the part's full range and letting the framework pick
+		 * means it picks the minimum, and this rail was measured running
+		 * at 1504 mV with a consumer attached. Everything on it wants
+		 * 1.8: the WCN3990's vddio below, and in the vendor tree the
+		 * WCD937x's cdc-vdd-rxtx and cdc-vddpx, the SAR sensor's cap_vdd
+		 * and -- on the boards that declare it -- the NFC controller.
+		 * The vendor pins it with qcom,init-voltage = <1800000>, which
+		 * mainline has no equivalent for; narrowing the constraint is how
+		 * it is said here.
+		 */
 		pm6125_l9: l9 {
-			regulator-min-microvolt = <1504000>;
-			regulator-max-microvolt = <2000000>;
+			regulator-min-microvolt = <1800000>;
+			regulator-max-microvolt = <1800000>;
 		};
 
 		pm6125_l10: l10 {
